extStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"3vqzv2918","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-4liek","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"a8pp62919","nodes":[],"type":"PARAGRAPH"},{"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}},"style":{},"id":"viewer-je8b","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Please try to wait at least 45 minutes before slicing into this loaf if you can. You will avoid losing too much moisture as steam which will dry out the entire loaf rather rapidly.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"background":"var(--ricos-custom-p-background-color,unset)","foreground":""}}]}}],"type":"PARAGRAPH"}],"metadata":{"version":1.0,"createdTimestamp":"2023-02-18T22:19:23.782Z","updatedTimestamp":"2023-02-18T22:19:23.782Z","id":"26b50aa1-0b28-44c8-b5b5-591fb30c1560"},"documentStyle":{}},"instructions":"Add the dry ingredients to a stand mixer bowl and mix them together for a few seconds.\n\n \n\nNow add the sourdough starter and begin mixing on low speed slowly adding the warm water in a stream until the dough comes together and pulls away from the sides of the mixing bowl. Start with 1 cup of water and add a tablespoon at a time until the dough comes together.\n\n \n\nMix on low for about 5-7 minutes or until the dough seems soft and elastic. If the dough seems too wet add more flour a few tablespoons at a time until it's just sticky. \n\n \n\nAfter the dough has mixed and kneaded in your mixer, turn it out onto a well floured board and flour your hands. Knead the dough by hand a few times and then form it into a ball as illustrated above. it shouldn't really be sticky any more.\n\n \n\nPlace the dough to rise in an oiled bowl covered with plastic wrap until it has nearly doubled in size. This takes 60-90 minutes in my kitchen. \n\n \n\nScrape the dough back out onto your floured board and knead it a few times as before but this time place it into a well floured banneton or a basket or bowl lined with a floured thin cotton towel to rise once more.\n\n \n\nWhile the dough is proofing this second time, place a large dutch oven into your oven and preheat it to 450 degrees F.\n\n \n\nAfter about 45-60 minutes the dough should be nearly doubled in size. Flip the dough out onto a parchment sheet generously sprinkled with cornmeal or semolina flour.\n\n \n\nTake the dutch oven out of the 450 degree oven and set it on the stove top or other safe spot.\n\n \n\nScore your loaf as illustrated above.\n\n \n\nCarefully remove the lid of the extremely hot dutch oven with on oven mitt and then lift the scored loaf using the paper and place it, parchment paper and all, into the preheated dutch oven.\n\n \n\nReplace the lid using your oven mitt and place the dutch oven back into your oven.\n\n \n\nReduce the temperature to 400 degrees and bake for 50 minutes.\n\n \n\nPlease try to wait at least 45 minutes before slicing into this loaf if you can. You will avoid losing too much moisture as steam which will dry out the entire loaf rather rapidly.","title":"Rosemary Garlic Sourdough Bread"}}],"pagingMetadata":{"count":1,"offset":0,"tooManyToCount":false,"cursors":{"next":""},"hasNext":true}}